and smooth execution.  I remember that game.
     (Apologies for the irrelevant comment.)

     Ob-nethack: I disapprove of class specializations 
(with regard to magic, weapons and so forth) for several 
reasons: 
1) Several classes would undoubtedly be left out in 
the crunch, and would fade from use in the game, most 
likely. 
2) The dev team would end up having to juggle around about 
ten different concepts of play balance, which is virtually 
impossible.
3) The game right now is geared towards combat more than 
spells, and things would have to shift before wizards 
would get anywhere in the new system.

     I do support Dave Hairston's notion of wizards 
finding more magic lying around and running into more 
magic-using creatures (although that cloak of MR would 
kinda make life a free ride for wizards-- unless we used 
the AD&D concept of a certain percentage of MR, adjusted 
by 5% for level differences).  Also, wizards should 
probably get a considerable bonus for retainment of spells 
(say, three times as many uses for a given spellbook), and 
perhaps the pwr stat should be dragged back into the 
relevant portion of the game.  (Food usage for 
spellcasting might be reduced significantly to 
compensate.)  Identify should use up at LEAST 15 power.


